        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the easiest DIY landscaping project for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Defining bed edges is the easiest high-impact project. It needs a spade, a hose to lay out curves, and optional edging, and it can be finished in an afternoon. A crisp line between lawn and bed makes the whole yard look more deliberate, and it is simple to adjust if you change your mind.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does DIY landscaping cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the scale. Edging, mulch and a few plants can be done on a modest budget, while pavers, retaining walls and irrigation cost more. Prices vary by region and supplier, so get quotes for bulk materials and price each project separately before committing to a full plan.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use landscape fabric under mulch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use it under gravel paths and around permanent shrubs, where it limits weeds for years. In beds you replant, thick mulch or overlapping cardboard usually works better, because roots and soil work through fabric over time and it becomes a nuisance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How deep should mulch be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Extension services commonly recommend about two to three inches. Thicker layers can smother roots and hold too much moisture, and thinner ones let weeds through. Keep mulch back from tree trunks and stems so the bark stays dry and healthy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is the best time to do landscaping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Spring and early autumn are the most forgiving seasons for planting, since roots establish before extreme heat or cold. Hardscape such as paths and edging can be done whenever the ground is workable, but pavers and sealers need dry weather and suitable temperatures.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a permit for landscaping projects?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Simple planting and mulching rarely do, but walls above a certain height, major grading, drainage changes and some fences often need approval. Check with your local building department and any homeowners association before starting, and call your utility locating service before digging.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What landscaping ideas add the most curb appeal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clean edges, fresh mulch, a defined path to the door, lighting and a few well-chosen plants near the entrance usually make the biggest visible difference. Most of these are inexpensive and can be done over a weekend, which makes them a better first step than large, costly projects.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

